# Alladin PCB — AI working rules (MCP)

- Only these MCP tools exist — read-only: `board_summary`, `get_footprints`,
  `get_nets`, `list_parts`, `check_board`, `get_routing_scene`, `probe_route`,
  `probe_placement`, `suggest_route` (needs write access only with `commit=true`);
  write: `new_board`, `download_lcsc_part`, `place_footprint`, `move_footprint`,
  `place_parts`, `move_parts`, `remove_footprint`, `connect_pins`, `disconnect_pin`,
  `add_pin_stitching_via`, `rename_net`, `save_board`, `commit_route`,
  `ripup_wire`.
- Never create, edit, or parse board `.json` by hand — use MCP / the GUI.
- Start with `board_summary`. After each batch of changes, verify your own
  work with `check_board` before reporting done.
- Write tools need the GUI launched with `--allow-ai-write`.
- Placement coordinates are mm with the origin at the board center
  (+x right, +y down). `list_parts` gives the exact template names
  `place_footprint` / `place_parts` want.
- Floorplan: prefer `probe_placement` (optional `search_radius_mm`) then
  `place_parts` / `move_parts` (atomic, one Ctrl+Z, optional `pins` net map,
  reply includes `open_bridges` score). Avoid N× single place + blind spiral
  retries.
- A placement/move refusal names the violated JLCPCB DFM rule — pick a
  different spot and retry; never try to bypass the gate.
- `new_board` refuses to discard an open board unless `replace_current=true`
  — ask the human before passing that.
- Copper routing uses the same clearance gates as the GUI's manual 45°
  router. Preferred: `suggest_route` — a server-side octilinear A*
  (H/V/45° legs only, no 90° corners, no vias, single layer) whose
  result is commit-ready; pass `commit=true` to lay it in one call.
  Fallback for hand-tuned aesthetics: `get_routing_scene` → propose
  polylines → `probe_route` (batch) → `commit_route` → `check_board`.
  On blockage, rip up blocking copper, try the other layer, adjust
  `step_mm`, or route manually. Zone fill stays in the GUI.
- `connect_pins`/`disconnect_pin` treat a multi-pad pin (e.g. a thermal
  pad split into several same-numbered paste pads) as one electrical
  pin: every sibling pad joins/leaves the net together.
- `add_pin_stitching_via` never puts a via on or within clearance of any
  solder pad, same-net included (reflow hazard): crowded pins (e.g. an
  EP pad grid) are skipped rather than compromised — connect them with
  short traces to a freer spot and stitch there.
- Routes keep a 1.0mm comfort margin from the board edge by default;
  pass `edge_margin_mm` (min 0.2, the fab limit) only when space truly
  forces the route closer. Mounting holes carry an automatic
  screw-head keep-out (radius = full drill diameter) that all copper
  respects.
- Every write lands in the GUI's Ctrl+Z undo history, so the human can take
  back anything you do.
